IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `da-claims`
IMPORT `da-qa`

-- Defamation Act 2005 (WA) -- cases for the Q&A layer (da-qa.l4): every exported question, for every role and every
-- situation.
--
-- Each question is asked for the role it is not about, with every other input an ASSUMEd value that has none: the
-- answer must be `does not arise for you`, and reading any of those inputs would stop the evaluation. Each question that
-- takes a situation input is asked, for its own role, with each value under which the situation does not hold and every
-- input after it ASSUMEd. For the baseline's seat (the person defamed) the answers are also asserted equal to the
-- baseline's own export on the same facts. Expected values are worked by hand from the deposited text (the quotations in
-- da-claims.l4). Daniel's facts (d062: the false post about Jarrah Legal Technologies Pty Ltd, 40 full-time
-- equivalents, the concerns notice, the offer 10 days after it) are the baseline's, illustrative where it says so; the
-- publisher's cases put the same facts from the other side.
--
-- Generated by gen_da.py in the session scratchpad. BASELINE. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cases -- the Q&A layer`

ASSUME `an unasked fact` IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME `an unasked number` IS A NUMBER
ASSUME `an unasked reputation` IS A `Whose reputation`
ASSUME `an unasked subject` IS A `Who the matter is about`

`as the person defamed` MEANS `I, or my company, have been defamed, or say so`
`as the publisher` MEANS `I published something that someone says defames them`

-- Can my company sue for defamation
-- Jarrah in 2040 (d062): for profit with 40 staff, no; in its first year with 3: yes; a small subsidiary: no; a charity of 200: yes; a council: no; 10 full-time equivalents: no; 9.5: yes (s 9(2), (3)).
